# Shot-source upstream revision

What framing and realizing one reviewed screenplay scene or brief shot proves wrong in its authored and design parents, and what is repaired there. Every selected shot or acceptance export answers for the unit and source interfaces it actually consumes.

## Parent revision from shot work {#parent-revision-from-shot-work}

Shot work tests whether the promised observation fits one reproducible camera, light, duration, subject state, and acceptance condition. Report the exact parent defect it exposes: action that cannot fit the frame or time, an observation that cannot falsify the promise, missing subject or environment state, incompatible source interfaces, or screenplay content with no realizable audiovisual form. Repair the earliest screenplay, brief, settings, design, or source owner; the shot may not invent narrative meaning, asset capability, or acceptance criteria.

`@evidence` names every upstream target repaired and the framing, timing, state, or acceptance test that exposed it. When the export exposes no defect, `@evidenceExclude` names its actual screenplay or brief unit and the concrete source interfaces, camera, timing, state, and predicate realized as written. A parentless export or generic render-success claim covers nothing.

Review question: what did implementing this shot export prove wrong in its actual authored or design parents, or which concrete parent decisions did it test and find sufficient?

Sources: [NASA on requirement-specific verification conditions and methods](https://www.nasa.gov/reference/system-engineering-handbook-appendix/); [SMPTE ST 2067-3 on composition playlists as explicit track-resource mappings](https://pub.smpte.org/doc/st2067-3/20220511-pub/)
